  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST state that exact text the user supplied, or explicit agreement earlier in the conversation, counts as agreement, and that a general request to make the change does not.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST state two things. First, exact text the user supplied, or explicit agreement earlier in the conversation, counts as agreement. Second, a general request to make the change does not.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_says_what_counts_as_agreement]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", ...)`. That helper runs `assert_text` over `bundled_skill(\"habeas-requirements\")`, which is the collapsed text of `SKILL.md` plus its reference files. `assert_text` asserts that the text contains the phrase. The phrase is \"Exact text the user supplied, or explicit agreement earlier in the conversation, counts; a general request to build the feature does not\".\n\nThat single phrase covers both clauses: what counts, and the negative for a general request. A skill that dropped either clause would fail the assertion. The phrase appears in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the Workflow section. A search found it there, so the expected text matches the real skill.\n\nThe test says \"build the feature\" where the requirement says \"make the change\". The requirement fixes no exact wording, so this is not a gap.\n\nThe requirement has no further conditions, modes or boundaries. I found no marking gap and no assertion gap.",
